=== P3 two-session concurrency demos ===
race_demos.py -- concurrency demos for Ibom Crown result processing
database: resultsys   (Unix socket, no password, no TCP)

========================================================================
PART 1 -- the BUG: write skew under REPEATABLE READ
========================================================================
  A: committed  (saw 1 pending, approved 1)
  B: committed  (inserted a new PENDING row)
  Final batch status : PUBLISHED
  PENDING rows in that batch: 1
  >>> BUG REPRODUCED: a PUBLISHED batch contains an unapproved result.

========================================================================
PART 2 -- trigger constraint fix WHERE ONE APPLIES (A publishes and commits FIRST, then B tries to insert)
========================================================================
  A: published + committed
  B: blocked by trigger (SQLSTATE P0001)
  Final batch status : PUBLISHED
  PENDING rows in that batch: 0
  >>> When A commits BEFORE B acts, the INSERT-time trigger closes the hole.

========================================================================
PART 3 -- the FIX: SERIALIZABLE + retry on SQLSTATE 40001
========================================================================
  A final: committed on attempt 1 (approved 2 pending result(s) in retry)
  B: committed (inserted PENDING)
  Final batch status : PUBLISHED
  PENDING rows in that batch: 0
  >>> FIX CONFIRMED: no unapproved result in the PUBLISHED batch.

========================================================================
PART 4 -- COMPARISON ONLY: SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on result_batch
========================================================================
  A: committed (published after holding lock)  (locked status: APPROVED)
  B: failed: 40001
  >>> FOR UPDATE serialises the two officers: B blocks until A is done.
      This is heavier than SERIALIZABLE+retry; kept here for comparison only.

========================================================================
PART 5 -- READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ
========================================================================
  READ COMMITTED : before=0, after=1  (saw the new row)
  REPEATABLE READ: before=0, after=0  (snapshot frozen -- did NOT see the new row)
  >>> RC re-reads per statement; RR uses the snapshot frozen at BEGIN.
      The write-skew bug exists because RR's trigger SELECT cannot see a
      concurrently inserted row, so it under-counts PENDING results.

========================================================================
PART 6 -- deadlock (40P01) + the fixed-id-order version
========================================================================
  DEADLOCK version  A: failed: SQLSTATE 40P01 (ERROR:  deadlock detected
DETAIL:  Process 17066 waits for ShareLock on transaction 847; blocked by )
  DEADLOCK version  B: committed (no deadlock)
  FIXED ORDER  A (r1 then r2): committed
  FIXED ORDER  B (r1 then r2): committed
  >>> Updating shared rows in a consistent id order removes the deadlock.

